A||||17||ChadMan||Bad brake caliper...what would you do?||||9||
Z||000000||ChadMan||10-15-2000||04:57 PM||||I installed new parking brake cables on my 88 Coupe yesterday and noticed some brake-fluid spots inside the left rear wheel.  I checked out the caliper and found a leak around the bleeder screw.  I removed the screw, inspected it, then reinstalled it, and now fluid gushes from around the screw when the pedal is depressed - even when the screw is fully seated!<P>I'm pi$$ed because I replaced all 4 calipers in early June with rebuilt ones from the Fiero Store.  I could find nothing wrong with the bleeder screw and the caliper is not visibly cracked or damaged.  I'm stumped and frustrated as all hell.<P>What would you do?  Ask the Fiero Store for a free replacement?  (They might tell me to get bent since the part gave me 120 days of trouble-free service.)  Buy one locally and get the car back on the road ASAP?<P>I have spent more than $600 on brake parts this year and my Fiero is sitting undriveable in my father's garage.  AAAAAAAAAAARRRRRGH<P>(And guess what?  Even with all new cables, new rear calipers and the adjustment tightened all the way, my parking brake still doesn't work worth a crap.)<P>||||reg||9
Z||000001||ThatRickGuy||10-15-2000||06:34 PM||||I'd say start cheep. bleeder screws are only like $.50 at napa, just check and see if that could be the problem. if that doesn't help, you could see (I have no idea if this would work) about finding a slightly larger bleader screw and re-taping the hole... no idea on that one. but if it is a crack in the caliper, I'd send it back to where you got it, cast iron isn't something that just 'breaks' that easily. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Bob Englert||10-15-2000||07:04 PM||||The Fiero calipers are aluminum.  Could you have overtightened the bleed screw and damaged the threads?<P>I ask because I ruined 2 calipers when puting new brakes on my daughter's 87 coupe by overtightening the banjo bolt to the brake hose and stripped the threads in the caliper housing.  The aluminum caliper is a lot softer than the cast iron ones.  I have learned to tighten just enough on the aluminum.<P>This is one reason I am considering the Grand Am brake upgrade on the front of my new (to me) 87 GT.  I would feel much better about tightening the bolts in cast iron.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||batboy||10-15-2000||10:10 PM||||Any threads in aluminum are easy to bugger up. The key here is: what did you do or mess up while installing the e-brake cables? Surely you didn't open the bleeder valve? Maybe it got bumped and damaged? I'm with Rick, try slapping a new bleeder in first. Like Bob says, careful not to overtighten.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Seanh||10-15-2000||10:30 PM||||If that does not work, I would contact the Fiero Store. I would think they should be able to help you find the problem or get you some new cal's. I have never had any problems with their products so I cant tell you for sure if they would get some new one's, but Im sure they will help you.<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
Z||000005||william_thorniii||10-16-2000||08:46 AM||||Chad,<P>If all else fails... perhaps teflon tape might help you out.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||3
Z||000006||ChadMan||10-16-2000||09:22 AM||||Guys,<P>Thanks for the advice - I did try wrapping the bleeder screw in teflon tape (didn't help).  But I didn't try a new bleeder screw - I was tired and sore after installing a new catalytic convertor and parking brake cables and didn't feel like working on the car any more!<P>I'm about 99% sure I didn't overtighten the screw.  However, when I first tried to loosen it, it was impossible to turn by hand even after breaking it loose with a wrench.  I'm wondering if it was cross-threaded from the rebuild shop because I didn't mess with the bleeder screw during the cable operation.  The strange thing is, I don't remember any problems bleeding that caliper during the original brake install back in June.<P>I will swing by NAPA on my lunch hour today and try a new screw tonight (insert joke here).  If that doesn't work, I'll call the Fiero Store and see if they'll send me a new caliper.<P>Thanks again!||||reg||1
Z||000007||ThatRickGuy||10-16-2000||10:06 AM||||woo, aluminum calipers? I really have to knock some of that rust off of them. I've been working so hard on the engine stuff I haven't paid much attention to the brakes, that will be the next project tho.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Hartz||10-16-2000||09:23 PM||||ChadMan,<P>Did you get this one sorted?  I am having similar problems with brake calipers from the Fiero Store, and getting frustrated.  Let me know what happens!<P>Best of luck,<BR>Mike||||reg||5
Z||000009||theogre||10-16-2000||09:32 PM||||Guys,,,<P>They make bleeder repair kits just for this problem.<P>The only things you can do if FS won't replace them is the repair kit or drill and tap for new oversize bleeder screws. (The repair kit is an insert, usually brass, with it's own bleeder.)||||reg||1
Z||000010||fiero56||10-16-2000||10:10 PM||||In defense of FS, I replaced the entire front braking system(pads, calipers, m/c)and haven't had any troubles. I haven't done the rears yet $$$$$$$.<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pffsig1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||ChadMan||10-16-2000||10:45 PM||||Fiero's still in Dad's garage - I'm still driving Mom's '78 Caprice.<P>Here's what I plan to do:<P>1)  Tomorrow night:  Replace the bleeder screw with another standard-size one.  If that doesn't fix it (and I don't think it will...)<P>2)  Wednesday morning:  Call the Fiero Store and ask for a free replacement caliper.  Not sure if they'll help me out or not since the caliper has been on the car for 4 months.  If FS won't help me out...<P>3)  Wednesday afternoon:  Go to NAPA and order a left-rear caliper ($76.50).  Thanks for the advice on the bleeder kit, Ogre, but I just want my Fiero back in a big hurry.  I don't think the NAPA calipers come with sliders and all that junk but I can transfer from the existing caliper.<P>I will keep you guys posted - thanks!<P>And Hartz, I'd like to hear about your FS caliper troubles.  I have $500 in these damn calipers (plus $100 in pads/cables) and I really, really don't want to hear that they have a chronic defect.<p>[This message has been edited by ChadMan (edited 10-16-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||ChadMan||10-17-2000||08:04 PM||||Sometimes you need somebody to point out the obvious...<P>I took my bleeder screw to Autozone, where the guy pointed out that what I thought was the bleeder is actually the bleeder AND the seat jammed together!  Instead of the bleeder screw breaking free from the seat, the seat broke free from the caliper.<P>So I put some Loctite Red on the threads, cleaned up the caliper and reinstalled the seat/screw assembly.  Hopefully tomorrow the screw will break free from the seat, the seat will stay put in the caliper and make a good seal and I'll be back in business.<P>Stay tuned...||||reg||11
Z||000013||ChadMan||10-18-2000||02:04 PM||||Well, the Loctite did affix the bleeder seat to the caliper where it belongs...however, the seat still doesn't make a good seal.  I'm thinking it was cross-threaded at the rebuild shop.<P>Called the Fiero Store, and they were very helpful.  They're going to send me a new caliper by UPS 2nd day air.  They're going to charge me for it, but refund the full cost of the replacement caliper as soon as I return the defective one.  So my only cost will be for shipping.<P>Looks like the Fiero Store is going to come through for me again!  I'll keep you posted...when the new caliper is safely on the car and my VISA card has been credited, then I will be one happy Fieroholic.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Hartz||10-20-2000||06:30 PM||||ChadMan, <P>Hey - I wasn't trying to imply anything about the Fiero Store, or any of their products.  I love the Fiero Store.  I was just simply looking for ideas on a similar problem I was having.<P>I wasn't keen on the idea of going through the hassle of returning the brakes, though, so I found a small gasket made of material impervious (hopefully) to the brake fluid.  I placed it under the copper gasket, and torqued to specs.  Multiple trips around town, and no leaks so far (knock on wood!).<P>Hope your car turns out OK as well.  Take care,<P>Hartz<BR>||||reg||10
Z||000015||ChadMan||10-21-2000||05:29 PM||||Well, I'm very unhappy with the Fiero Store right now.<P>I told them I needed the caliper before the weekend, so they sent it UPS 2nd day air.  It never showed up, so I called them today and they said their UPS computer was down so they couldn't track the shipment.  So I asked for the tracking number, then had to go run some errands with my wife, then punched in the tracking number.  Turns out the guy gave me the tracking number for the damn e-brake cables I ordered two weeks ago - not the tracking number for the caliper!<P>So I have no caliper, no idea when it will arrive or even if they sent it, and no way to check until Monday morning.<P>Worse yet, my wife's grandmother was killed in a traffic accident and the showing is Monday and the funeral Tuesday - so even if the caliper showed up Monday I couldn't put it on until Wednesday night.<P>DAMN do I wish I had an 84-87 instead of my 88.  I could have bought the calipers here in town in the first place and not had to screw around with mail order and all the associated delays and hassles.||||reg||8
Z||000016||ChadMan||10-23-2000||09:51 AM||||Just got off the phone with the Fiero Store - turns out they goofed and sent the caliper UPS Ground instead of 2nd day air as promised - so it should arrive today.  They're going to refund all my shipping charges, so I'm merely annoyed, not super-pi$$ed anymore.<P>The proof will be in whether the new caliper is defective or not.  If it works, I'm happy.  If it doesn't, Hartz, I will probably email you to find out more about your fix and try that.<P>Can ChadMan slip away from a houseful of distraught, grieving Italians, install his caliper, and return without enraging his wife?  Find out in our next episode!||||reg||1
Z||000017||ChadMan||10-24-2000||05:20 PM||||What an emotion-filled day.  I went to my wife's grandmother's funeral and served as a pallbearer, gave everybody my heartfelt condolences (she really was a sweet lady), then ducked out and went to my dad's to install my new caliper.<P>So far, so good!  The pedal's firm and the car stops straight.  What's more, the parking brake is now semi-functional...for some reason it doesn't take as long a pull to actuate the new caliper.  Tomorrow I'll send the old caliper back to the Fiero Store - once they credit my VISA this ordeal will be over!<P>I can't get over how much I missed this car.||||reg||10
